      "problem": "$2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{{e}^{4}})=$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we rewrite $\\frac{1}{{e}^{4}}$ as a power of $e$ (the base of the natural logarithm) as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{{e}^{4}}={e}^{-4}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have:\n\n$$\n2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{{e}^{4}})=2\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{-4})\n$$\n\nThe natural logarithm reverses the action of the exponent. The principle is:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{x})=x\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $x=-4.$ Therefore:\n\n$$\n2\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{-4})=2\\cdot (-4)=-8\n$$",
